" style=\"height:-0.15em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.7em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mord mathrm\">S</span></span></span></span></span>","key":"pxdMBeNIPp"},{"type":"text","value":"). These factors affect how magma erupts and the type of volcano it will form when it solidifies. Note that the amount of silica in magma and its viscosity are positively correlated. Magma can contain 1% to 10% dissolved gases, much like a soda contains dissolved ","position":{"start":{"line":72,"column":1},"end":{"line":72,"column":1}},"key":"tGwlUIeNds"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"\\ce{CO2}","position":{"start":{"line":72,"column":1},"end":{"line":72,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></mrow><msub><mpadded width=\"0px\"><mphantom><mi>X</mi></mphantom></mpadded><mpadded height=\"0px\"><mn>2</mn></mpadded></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ce{CO2}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">CO</span></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord rlap\"><span class=\"inner\"><span class=\"mord\" style=\"color:transparent;\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.07847em;color:transparent;\">X</span></span></span><span class=\"fix\"></span></span></span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:-0.15em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.7em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>","key":"HTShjXyofP"},{"type":"text","value":".","position":{"start":{"line":72,"column":1},"end":{"line":72,"column":1}},"key":"zDKxkvZWwj"}],"key":"SmvUZyaVZj"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":74,"column":1},"end":{"line":74,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Igneous rock is formed when liquid rock freezes into a solid rock. This molten material is called magma when it is in the ground and lava when it is on the surface. Only the Earth’s outer core is liquid; the Earth’s mantle and crust is naturally solid. However, there are a few minor pockets of magma that form near the surface where geologic processes cause melting. It is this magma that becomes the source for volcanoes and igneous rocks. This chapter will describe the classification of igneous rocks, the unique processes that form magmas, types of volcanoes and volcanic processes, volcanic hazards, and igneous landforms.","position":{"start":{"line":74,"column":1},"end":{"line":74,"column":1}},"key":"mMrUIi413J"}],"key":"BbhGtzB5mH"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":76,"column":1},"end":{"line":76,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lava cools quickly on the surface of the earth and forms tiny microscopic crystals. These are known as fine-grained extrusive, or volcanic, igneous rocks. Extrusive rocks are often vesicular, filled with holes from escaping gas bubbles. Volcanism is the process in which lava is erupted. Depending on the properties of the lava that is erupted, the volcanism can be drastically different, from smooth and gentle to dangerous and explosive. This leads to different types of volcanoes and different volcanic hazards.","position":{"start":{"line":76,"column":1},"end":{"line":76,"column":1}},"key":"VaN2Z5y1gQ"}],"key":"gEm0V4XXe1"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":78,"column":1},"end":{"line":78,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In contrast, magma that cools slowly below the earth’s surface forms larger crystals which can be seen with the naked eye. These are known as coarse-grained intrusive, or plutonic, igneous rocks. This relationship between cooling rates and grain sizes of the solidified minerals in igneous rocks is important for interpreting the rock’s geologic history.","position":{"start":{"line":78,"column":1},"end":{"line":78,"column":1}},"key":"n5sapF8BhA"}],"key":"PgvYDi4xiQ"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":80,"column":1},"end":{"line":80,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"link","url":"https://slcc.pressbooks.pub/introgeology/chapter/4-igneous-processes-and-volcanoes/","position":{"start":{"line":80,"column":1},"end":{"line":80,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Text Source","position":{"start":{"line":80,"column":1},"end":{"line":80,"column":1}},"key":"bG7PCXWVy1"}],"urlSource":"https://slcc.pressbooks.pub/introgeology/chapter/4-igneous-processes-and-volcanoes/","key":"gA4A3A3741"}],"key":"eH3ahtC75i"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":82,"column":1},"end":{"line":82,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Eruptions and Hazards","position":{"start":{"line":82,"column":1},"end":{"line":82,"column":1}},"key":"rHWZGcpofq"}],"identifier":"eruptions-and-hazards","label":"Eruptions and Hazards","html_id":"eruptions-and-hazards","implicit":true,"key":"mW5gcM4lb9"},{"type":"div","class":"container","children":[{"type":"iframe","class":"video","src":"https://www.youtube.com/embed/BCm6xTZj-vk","width":"100%","key":"FTDmilaUnF"}],"key":"zKunUnYBTO"},{"type":"div","class":"container","children":[{"type":"iframe","class":"video","src":"https://www.youtube.com/embed/69iUhlqFJFk","width":"100%","key":"hIWWNZzi72"}],"key":"qTbO2k4Gch"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":96,"column":1},"end":{"line":96,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Classification of Igneous Rocks","position":{"start":{"line":96,"column":1},"end":{"line":96,"column":1}},"key":"N57vM0u0E6"}],"identifier":"classification-of-igneous-rocks","label":"Classification of Igneous Rocks","html_id":"classification-of-igneous-rocks","implicit":true,"key":"hi9OAKXENL"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":98,"column":1},"end":{"line":98,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Igneous rocks are classified based on texture and composition. Texture describes the physical characteristics of the minerals, such as grain size. This relates to the cooling history of the molten magma from which it came. Composition refers to the rock’s specific mineralogy and chemical composition. Cooling history is also related to changes that can occur to the composition of igneous rocks.","position":{"start":{"line":98,"column":1},"end":{"line":98,"column":1}},"key":"s0wcvRPvZo"}],"key":"c0o2EuPFgc"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"position":{"start":{"line":100,"column":1},"end":{"line":100,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Texture","position":{"start":{"line":100,"column":1},"end":{"line":100,"column":1}},"key":"Z4bYazJWUj"}],"identifier":"texture","label":"Texture","html_id":"texture","implicit":true,"key":"ElHh62qt8x"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":102,"column":1},"end":{"line":102,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The two main textures found in igneous rocks – coarse-grained (","position":{"start":{"line":102,"column":1},"end":{"line":102,"column":1}},"key":"mo3PGq9aEm"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":102,"column":1},"end":{"line":102,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"phaneritic","position":{"start":{"line":102,"column":1},"end":{"line":102,"column":1}},"key":"gYW4m5p9fP"}],"key":"kXCA3tJ5oe"},{"type":"text","value":") and fine-grained (","position":{"start":{"line":102,"column":1},"end":{"line":102,"column":1}},"key":"TBPUUpOdxS"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":102,"column":1},"end":{"line":102,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"aphanitic","position":{"start":{"line":102,"column":1},"end":{"line":102,"column":1}},"key":"ANZLRXNnmR"}],"key":"tl4RRBwBWt"},{"type":"text","value":")- provide information about the volcanic history of the rock.","position":{"start":{"line":102,"column":1},"end":{"line":102,"column":1}},"key":"CGYHtyyXHj"}],"key":"exzXgYIEYt"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":104,"column":1},"end":{"line":104,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":104,"column":1},"end":{"line":104,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Phaneritic","position":{"start":{"line":104,"column":1},"end":{"line":104,"column":1}},"key":"UY8vpKM16x"}],"key":"A3Bv15gON0"},{"type":"text","value":" textured rocks have large crystals (the rock is coarse-grained). These rocks formed from slow-cooling magma underground, allowing time for crystals to grow large.","position":{"start":{"line":104,"column":1},"end":{"line":104,"column":1}},"key":"v2YrTkperI"}],"key":"bqNlyyC1u6"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":106,"column":1},"end":{"line":106,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":106,"column":1},"end":{"line":106,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Aphanitic","position":{"start":{"line":106,"column":1},"end":{"line":106,"column":1}},"key":"ir3e9E83lf"}],"key":"nkDseEgwxy"},{"type":"text","value":" textured rocks have small crystals (the rock is fine-grained) and appear uniform in color overall. These rocks formed from faster-cooling lava at Earth’s surface, so there was no time for the crystals to grow.","position":{"start":{"line":106,"column":1},"end":{"line":106,"column":1}},"key":"E4B46awuv9"}],"key":"rszoJxILcd"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/Granite_Rosa_Porriño-a4d35faeee0c109a4c84012fcb437dfd.jpg","alt":"Granite is a classic coarse-grained (phaneritic) intrusive igneous rock. The different colors are unique minerals. The black colors are likely two or three different minerals. Image source","data":{"altTextIsAutoGenerated":true},"key":"X3WIZGDJtJ","urlSource":"assets/Granite_Rosa_Porriño_-_es_(rotated).jpg"},{"type":"caption","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":113,"column":1},"end":{"line":113,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"captionNumber","kind":"figure","label":"granite-texture","identifier":"granite-texture","html_id":"granite-texture","enumerator":"3","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Figure ","key":"XLdDA81ItX"},{"type":"text","value":"3","key":"dtemJJyUEp"},{"type":"text","value":":","key":"vqY2UsFzOb"}],"template":"Figure %s:","key":"scJPb3or2K"},{"type":"text","value":"Granite is a classic coarse-grained (phaneritic) intrusive igneous rock. The different colors are unique minerals. The black colors are likely two or three different minerals. ","position":{"start":{"line":113,"column":1},"end":{"line":113,"column":1}},"key":"Sck28L8FEO"},{"type":"link","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Granite","position":{"start":{"line":113,"column":1},"end":{"line":113,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Image source","position":{"start":{"line":113,"column":1},"end":{"line":113,"column":1}},"key":"SPovYNSuOh"}],"urlSource":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Granite","data":{"page":"Granite","wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/","lang":"en"},"internal":false,"protocol":"wiki","key":"qOB29CGORM"}],"key":"OFq3mZRkfF"}],"key":"MoB0GpLI4T"}],"class":"margin-caption","label":"granite-texture","identifier":"granite-texture","enumerator":"3","html_id":"granite-texture","key":"gBqNVz4IFW"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":116,"column":1},"end":{"line":116,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"When lava is extruded onto the surface, or intruded into shallow fissures near the surface and cools, the resulting igneous rock is called extrusive or volcanic. Extrusive igneous rocks have a fine-grained or aphanitic texture, in which the grains are too small to see with the unaided eye. The fine-grained texture indicates the quickly cooling lava did not have time to grow large crystals. These tiny crystals can be viewed under a petrographic microscope. In some cases, extrusive lava cools so rapidly it does not develop crystals at all. This non-crystalline material is not classified as minerals, but as volcanic glass. This is a common component of volcanic ash and rocks like obsidian.","position":{"start":{"line":116,"column":1},"end":{"line":116,"column":1}},"key":"dExB2cRKwu"}],"key":"Nx7XbSnqUL"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/7e7f2f448c2337eb8c0224bf4cead322.jpeg","alt":"Basalt is a classic fine-grained extrusive igneous rock. This sample is mostly fine-grained groundmass with a few small green phenocrysts composed of olivine. Image source","data":{"altTextIsAutoGenerated":true},"key":"HIHsgUmxDq","urlSource":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Basalt_12_%2848674276333%29.jpg/1280px-Basalt_12_%2848674276333%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"type":"caption","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":123,"column":1},"end":{"line":123,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"captionNumber","kind":"figure","label":"basalt-texture","identifier":"basalt-texture","html_id":"basalt-texture","enumerator":"4","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Figure ","key":"OzyZKM26Ze"},{"type":"text","value":"4","key":"ALZMRyeakB"},{"type":"text","value":":","key":"STpv6QQbS9"}],"template":"Figure %s:","key":"DPGwv7AZPS"},{"type":"text","value":"Basalt is a classic fine-grained extrusive igneous rock. This sample is mostly fine-grained groundmass with a few small green phenocrysts composed of olivine. ","position":{"start":{"line":123,"column":1},"end":{"line":123,"column":1}},"key":"C2xV0lDFk6"},{"type":"link","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basalt","position":{"start":{"line":123,"column":1},"end":{"line":123,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Image source","position":{"start":{"line":123,"column":1},"end":{"line":123,"column":1}},"key":"Wk1pqZyEzn"}],"urlSource":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basalt","data":{"page":"Basalt","wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/","lang":"en"},"internal":false,"protocol":"wiki","key":"H9zZvFl2Bk"}],"key":"aI6UfqWW4L"}],"key":"zIUl9VAoxy"}],"class":"margin-caption","label":"basalt-texture","identifier":"basalt-texture","enumerator":"4","html_id":"basalt-texture","key":"M1RuRAPCMP"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":128,"column":1},"end":{"line":128,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"All magmas contain gases dissolved in solution called volatiles. As the magma rises to the surface, the drop in pressure causes the dissolved volatiles to come bubbling out of solution, like the fizz in an opened bottle of soda. The gas bubbles become trapped in the solidifying lava to create a vesicular texture, with the holes specifically called vesicles. The type of volcanic rock with common vesicles is called scoria.","position":{"start":{"line":128,"column":1},"end":{"line":128,"column":1}},"key":"IqkACTrYGP"}],"key":"XRTJ3H1i08"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"position":{"start":{"line":130,"column":1},"end":{"line":130,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Composition","position":{"start":{"line":130,"column":1},"end":{"line":130,"column":1}},"key":"uGPMxY1jWa"}],"identifier":"composition","label":"Composition","html_id":"composition","implicit":true,"key":"DOf8oppLhv"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":132,"column":1},"end":{"line":132,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Composition refers to a rock’s chemical and mineral make-up . For igneous rock, composition is divided into four groups: felsic, intermediate, mafic, and ultramafic. These groups refer to differing amounts of silica, iron, and magnesium found in the minerals that make up the rocks. It is important to realize these groups do not have sharp boundaries in nature, but rather lie on a continuous spectrum with many transitional compositions and names that refer to specific quantities of minerals. As an example, granite is a commonly-used term, but has a very specific definition which includes exact quantities of minerals like feldspar and quartz. Rocks labeled as ‘granite‘ in laymen applications can be several other rocks, including  syenite, tonalite, and monzonite. To avoid these complications, the following figure presents a simplified version of igneous rock nomenclature focusing on the four main groups, which is adequate for an introductory student.","position":{"start":{"line":132,"column":1},"end":{"line":132,"column":1}},"key":"I9cjqejtKY"}],"key":"wfoNGhRmcM"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":134,"column":1},"end":{"line":134,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Felsic refers to a predominance of the light-colored (felsic) minerals feldspar and silica in the form of quartz. These light-colored minerals have more silica as a proportion of their overall chemical formula. Minor amounts of dark-colored (mafic) minerals like amphibole and biotite mica may be present as well. Felsic igneous rocks are rich in silica (in the 65-75% range, meaning the rock would be 65-75% weight percent SiO2) and poor in iron and magnesium.","position":{"start":{"line":134,"column":1},"end":{"line":134,"column":1}},"key":"r0N8vIV8hZ"}],"key":"SUEQRH7JVX"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":136,"column":1},"end":{"line":136,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Intermediate is a composition between felsic and mafic.  It usually contains roughly-equal amounts of light and dark minerals, including light grains of plagioclase feldspar and dark minerals like amphibole.  It is intermediate in silica in the 55-60% range.","position":{"start":{"line":136,"column":1},"end":{"line":136,"column":1}},"key":"EZe9Jvdogw"}],"key":"W5WeaImIxl"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":138,"column":1},"end":{"line":138,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mafic refers to a abundance of ferromagnesian minerals (with magnesium and iron, chemical symbols Mg and Fe) plus plagioclase feldspar. It is mostly made of dark minerals like pyroxene and olivine, which are rich in iron and magnesium and relatively poor in silica. Mafic rocks are low in silica, in the 45-50% range.","position":{"start":{"line":138,"column":1},"end":{"line":138,"column":1}},"key":"DGYmIgRhqI"}],"key":"zVHp3B4GQ4"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":140,"column":1},"end":{"line":140,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ultramafic refers to the extremely mafic rocks composed of mostly olivine and some pyroxene which have even more magnesium and iron and even less silica. These rocks are rare on the surface, but make up peridotite, the rock of the upper mantle. It is poor in silica, in the 40% or less range.","position":{"start":{"line":140,"column":1},"end":{"line":140,"column":1}},"key":"WXYusz0paR"}],"key":"UrAGuEJyj5"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":142,"column":1},"end":{"line":142,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"link","url":"https://slcc.pressbooks.pub/introgeology/chapter/4-igneous-processes-and-volcanoes/","position":{"start":{"line":142,"column":1},"end":{"line":142,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Text Source","position":{"start":{"line":142,"column":1},"end":{"line":142,"column":1}},"key":"vTONAG3oRj"}],"urlSource":"https://slcc.pressbooks.pub/introgeology/chapter/4-igneous-processes-and-volcanoes/","key":"TQIDUmyF4l"}],"key":"LBQ37vhiq9"},{"type":"div","class":"container","children":[{"type":"iframe","class":"video","src":"https://www.youtube.com/embed/WSv3r3WwC0I","width":"100%","key":"Q1aguonBFZ"}],"key":"kHwxsGScyE"},{"type":"div","class":"container","children":[{"type":"iframe","class":"video","src":"https://www.youtube.com/embed/zEeL0J0PPyk","width":"100%","key":"EUjZPFBPkf"}],"key":"C37agYHWsg"}],"key":"daWsC1j3p6"}],"key":"dUlfbV6FYU"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}}}